  would silently lose them. On sender failure the queue is left completely intact and [38;5;153mflush[39m returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m; the caller
  retries whenever it likes. No timers, no retry loop, no backoff inside the tracker — scheduling belongs to whoever
  owns the backend, which doesn't exist yet. Nothing calls [38;5;153mflush[39m in this change; it's the seam, wired up when there's
  somewhere to send to.

  [1mTesting with [38;5;153mnode:test[39m.[0m One wrinkle worth designing around: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is a browser global and Node doesn't reliably
  provide it. So the tracker takes its storage as an injectable dependency defaulting to [38;5;153mglobalThis.localStorage[39m, and
  tests pass a [38;5;153mMap[39m-backed fake. That keeps tests dependency-free and hermetic — no browser, no jsdom. First tests cover
  the queue cap and eviction order, the password-key rejection, corrupt-JSON recovery, storage-throws fallback, and that
  flush preserves records added mid-flight.

  [1mOne decision left, because it changes the interface.[0m Hashing the username properly means
  [38;5;153mcrypto.subtle.digest("SHA-256", …)[39m, which is async — and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is synchronous today.

  - [1m[38;5;153mtrack()[39m returns a promise; [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls it without awaiting.[0m Gets real SHA-256. Costs an internal promise chain to
    serialize queue writes, since two overlapping async read-modify-writes would otherwise lose a record. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays
    synchronous and its return value is unchanged. [3mThis is the interface every future form is written against, so it's [0m
    [3mworth getting right now.[0m
  - [1m[38;5;153mtrack()[39m stays synchronous, using a small non-cryptographic hash (FNV-1a).[0m No races, no promise chain, simplest
    possible code. But a 32-bit non-crypto hash of a short username is brute-forceable in milliseconds, so the
    pseudonymisation is close to decorative.

  [1mMy recommendation: the async version.[0m You chose a pseudonymous ID to avoid storing identifiable data; FNV-1a doesn't
  actually deliver that, so the sync option quietly undercuts the decision you already made. The promise chain is about
  five lines. I'd also salt the hash with a per-deployment constant, which is what stops the "just hash every common
  username and compare" attack that a bare digest is still open to.

